Asbestosis
Respiratory System
Bibasilar crackles, Decades post-exposure, Pleural plaques

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-006
Reason for Visit: Acute worsening of chronic exertional dyspnea and onset of pleuritic chest pain requiring immediate medical review.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated new-onset pleuritic chest pain and increased work of breathing in a patient with known occupational lung disease. Urgent after-hours assessment was mandated to rule out acute pneumothorax or pulmonary embolism and manage severe pain, preventing respiratory deterioration before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My breathing has been getting worse for months, but tonight taking a deep breath feels like a knife in my side."
History of Present Illness: Symptoms evolved gradually over the past decade following extensive occupational asbestos exposure in the 1980s. Over the last 48 hours, the patient reports a marked increase in progressive exertional dyspnea and a persistent dry, non-productive cough. This evening, they developed sharp, pleuritic chest pain localized to the right lateral chest wall, severely limiting inspiratory effort and prompting the after-hours call.
Functional Status: Ambulating with a walking stick; currently chair-bound due to dyspnea and pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: High anxiety regarding disease progression; spouse struggling with physical care demands.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/88 mmHg | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 37.0 C | RR: 22 breaths/min | SpO2: 92% on room air | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Appears chronically unwell and anxious. Mild distress upon deep inspiration.
Physical Exam: Respiratory examination demonstrates markedly reduced chest wall expansion. Auscultation reveals fine, bibasilar end-inspiratory crackles ("Velcro-like"). Digital clubbing is evident on the hands. Signs of advanced cor pulmonale are absent. Patient is decades post-exposure, and known history includes pleural plaques on prior imaging.
Environmental Observations: Safe indoor environment; oxygen concentrator present but currently switched off.
Medication Reconciliation: Regular paracetamol ineffective for current pleuritic pain; using PRN salbutamol with no relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Asbestosis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Gradual onset of exertional dyspnea prior to pain, absence of hemoptysis or tachycardia >100.

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pain is strictly pleuritic and localized, without central crushing features or radiation.

Pneumonia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of fever, productive purulent sputum, or focal coarse crepitations.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute exacerbation of symptoms related to known asbestosis, primarily manifesting as new-onset severe pleuritic chest pain and acutely worsened dyspnea. The physical exam confirms bibasilar crackles and diminished chest expansion, without signs of acute cardiac failure or overwhelming infection. The severity of the pleuritic pain is impairing the patient's respiratory mechanics, causing localized splinting and dropping oxygen saturations to 92%. Delaying pain management and clinical assessment until the next in-hours period would predictably lead to severe atelectasis, hypoxemia, and the need for hospital admission. Therefore, an urgent after-hours home visit was strictly required to provide immediate analgesia and exclude acute life-threatening complications.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered NSAID (Ibuprofen 400mg) for acute pleuritic pain. Initiated continuous low-flow oxygen therapy at 2L/min via nasal prongs to maintain SpO2 >94%.
Positive Behavioral Change: Use an incentive spirometer every two hours during the day; performing this action provides visual feedback of lung expansion, which directly rewards and reinforces deep breathing efforts, thereby preventing localized lung collapse.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to community palliative care team for symptom management.
DME/Safety (Optional): Shower chair requested to reduce exertional fatigue.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 72 hours for ongoing pain management and formal pulmonary function testing.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department via ambulance (000) if chest pain becomes central and crushing, if coughing produces blood, or if confusion develops.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This assessment addresses the acute pain and dyspnea; underlying structural lung changes are irreversible.
Clinical Course: The condition is slowly progressive. The current pleuritic pain will likely respond to regular anti-inflammatory medication over the next 3-5 days.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 if your breathing becomes difficult while resting, if your lips turn blue, or if the pain spreads to your jaw or left arm.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The pain medication will reduce the inflammation around the lung lining (pleura), allowing you to take deeper, more effective breaths without splinting.
Actionable Steps: Take the prescribed pain relief strictly every 6 hours on a schedule; maintaining stable drug levels prevents the pain from returning, allowing you to comfortably complete your breathing exercises.

